WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:01.620
Speaker 1: But a major story, which we are
going to continue following another major

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:08.570
story this weekend of huge geopolitical and
foreign policy importance is that long time

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:15.759
Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu
of the Likud Party is finally out as prime

00:00:15.759 --> 00:00:24.380
minister, replaced by the incoming prime minister,
Naftali Bennett. In Israel. Much of what takes

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:32.020
place is coalition based. And so what we have
seen here is that by a single vote margin,

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:37.870
Naftali Bennett is in and Benjamin Netanyahu
is out because of the coalition that Bennett

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:43.480
was able to form. The New York Times reports
the long and divisive reign of Benjamin Netanyahu,

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:47.800
the dominant Israeli politician of the past
generation, officially ended on Sunday, at

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.870
least for the time being, as the country's
parliament gave its vote of confidence to

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:58.140
a precarious coalition government stitched
together by widely disparate anti Netanyahu

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:02.949
forces Israelis. Israel's parliament, the
Knesset, approved the new government by just

00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:08.890
a single vote 60 to 59, with one abstention
after his supporters cheered the announcement

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:14.710
of his election. Naftali Bennett then exchanged
a brief handshake with Netanyahu before walking

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:19.730
to the rostrum at the front of the parliamentary
chamber and taking the oath of office as Prime

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:24.330
Minister Yair Lapid, a centrist leader, is
set to take Mr. Bennett's place after two

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:29.060
years. If their government can hold together
that long, they lead an eight party alliance

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:34.170
ranging from left to right, from secular to
religious, that agrees on little but a desire

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:39.610
to oust Netanyahu, the longest serving leader
in the country's history and to end Israel's

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:44.770
lengthy political gridlock. In a speech made
before the confidence vote, Bennett hailed

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:50.090
his unlikely coalition as an essential antidote
to an intractable stalemate. We stopped the

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:54.390
train before the abyss, Bennett said. The
time has come for different leaders from all

00:01:54.390 --> 00:02:00.860
parts of the people to stop to stop this madness.
Now you will remember that Netanyahu, as the

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:05.640
article points out, and his right wing allies
from Likud and other far right parties did

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:09.579
everything they could. I I mean, I guess maybe
there was something else they could have done,

00:02:09.579 --> 00:02:13.549
something illegal. I don't know. They did
almost everything they could. They applied

00:02:13.549 --> 00:02:19.780
pressure on other right wing opposition lawmakers.
They were trying to peel them away from possibly

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:25.549
being on the Bennett side, but ultimately
unable to do it. The question, of course,

00:02:25.549 --> 00:02:32.989
now is what happens next? It's important to
understand that while Naftali Bennett is not

00:02:32.989 --> 00:02:40.319
a Likud Knick, the party Likud of Benjamin
Netanyahu, there is this political alliance,

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:45.849
Yamina, which is an alliance of right wing
parties, including what was originally the

00:02:45.849 --> 00:02:51.749
new right and some other parties. And we will
see ultimately how it shakes out in practice.

00:02:51.749 --> 00:02:55.969
Now, it is still a right wing coalition and
it is still Naftali Bennett is still a right

00:02:55.969 --> 00:03:00.680
winger. And it is a real question mark as
to as to what progress if. And he will be

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:05.829
able to be made in terms of the Israeli-Palestinian
conflict now on the other side, as I said

00:03:05.829 --> 00:03:12.230
before, it is a requirement, but it is necessary
but not sufficient to remove Benjamin Netanyahu,

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:16.670
is there? If there is going to be any serious
chance that peace talks that could be productive

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:23.469
and and permanent or semi-permanent, as we
may call it, on the other side of that, Hamas,

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:29.319
we know simply cannot be, will not be. They
refused to be. There is no way that Hamas

00:03:29.319 --> 00:03:34.950
will be a party to any kind of serious peace
talks. And Hamas seems potentially as ingrained

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:42.180
as ever in Gaza. Hamas, if you want to understand
sort of the outrageous dynamics of what's

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:49.290
going on there, Hamas recently awarded Al-Jazeera
an award which Al-Jazeera gladly accepted

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:54.980
for their coverage of the recent conflict
there. And for anybody who has legitimate

00:03:54.980 --> 00:04:02.010
criticism of the way that the recent flare
up with Gaza was covered by certain media

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:07.889
outlets, but does not have concerns about
the coverage of, for example, Al-Jazeera,

00:04:07.889 --> 00:04:12.379
which was recently given an award by Hamas.
You're sort of missing half of the picture.

00:04:12.379 --> 00:04:18.300
So I remain extremely skeptical as to the
progress that will be made in the next two

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:23.650
years, serious progress when it comes to the
Israeli-Palestinian conflict. But removing

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:30.360
Benjamin Netanyahu as prime minister was step
zero, point one. It was a necessary step.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:35.580
It is not sufficient. And we will now see
what happens next. It would be amazing if

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:44.300
there were able to be at least some initial
hypothetical guidelines set out for some kind

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:48.700
of serious path forward. But something that's
important to understand is, you know, one

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:56.599
of the risks of. Casually following the internal
politics of other countries is no matter who

00:04:56.599 --> 00:05:01.659
you follow, like whether you're following
The New York Times and The Times of Israel

00:05:01.659 --> 00:05:06.960
or whether you're following Al Jazeera for
your understanding of the Israeli-Palestinian

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:11.740
conflict, you sometimes miss the forest for
the trees. And one of the things that is really

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:17.680
important to understand is that within Israel,
meaning to Israelis, the Israeli-Palestinian

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:23.190
conflict does not actually rank particularly
high in terms of the concerns that the Israeli

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:28.570
people have for what they want a prime minister
or the new prime minister to focus on. It's

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:33.180
mostly internal issues. It's internal issues
related to economics, its internal issues

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:40.430
related to education and social services and
infrastructure, much like in the United States.

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:44.699
And it's not different in that sense. And
this is something that is really missed when

00:05:44.699 --> 00:05:51.820
you look at Israel and the Israeli-Palestinian
conflict through any kind of Western media,

00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:56.120
whether it's hard right. Or whether it's hard
left or whether it's centrist, our lens tends

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:02.819
to be foreign policy of Israel and the Israeli-Palestinian
conflict, much like in the United States foreign

00:06:02.819 --> 00:06:10.319
policy. We listen in the middle of a completely
hairbrained war with Iraq. In 2004, George

00:06:10.319 --> 00:06:15.840
W. Bush still was reelected. And the war in
Iraq, which is like we went to war with the

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:21.550
wrong country over a lie about the connection
between Saddam and 9/11, that's insane. And

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:26.870
even that only got the war in Iraq to a reasonably
important issue for Americans. The primary

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:32.159
issues were still domestic, economic, etc.
It's no different than Israel. And I think

00:06:32.159 --> 00:06:37.870
that that's a really important detail to understand,
as anybody from outside of Israel tries to

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:41.960
follow and understand what it is that's going
on there. So Netanyahu out. That's the good

